# Keep the evaluation ledger local; it is generated data and too large for GitHub history.
reports/intelligence/evaluation_ledger.jsonl
# RUNTIME EXECUTION STATE, never source. The ledger is the record of real
# orders and `execution_state` is the worker's heartbeat; both are written per
# process on Render's disk, and a copy from someone's laptop committed here
# would masquerade as a cold-start artifact production could read. Added
# 2026-08-24 after a test run swept both into two commits via `git add -A`.
reports/intelligence/execution_ledger.json
reports/intelligence/execution_state.json
reports/intelligence/kalshi_markets.json
reports/intelligence/kalshi_market_history.json
# THE BANKROLL, and the worst of this list to commit by accident. It is the
# STORED tier of `resolve_settings`, which outranks env AND default -- so a
# copy from a laptop landing here would set production's stake sizing to
# whatever a local test last wrote, and no environment variable could override
# it. Added 2026-08-26 after a `POST /portfolio/settings` round-trip test wrote
# bankroll=1000/max_positions=25 into this exact path.
reports/intelligence/portfolio_settings.json
reports/intelligence/evaluation_ledger_chunks/
# Same class as the chunks above: a per-date JSONL ledger the shortlist WRITES
# at runtime (`clv_openings`/`openings_records` in its payload). In production
# it lives on Render's disk; the only copies that ever appear in a checkout are
# test byproduct -- the one that prompted this rule held 7 rows of `event_id:
# evt-1` against real team names, which is worse than useless in a CLV ledger.
reports/intelligence/clv_openings/
reports/intelligence/archive/
reports/intelligence/board_snapshot*.json
reports/intelligence/query_state_cache.json

